[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r7
diagnosis_text:
The failure is not on R6. The affected subsystem is OSPF on R7: its OSPF daemon (ospfd) is down, which broke the adjacency on the R6–R7 link. Direct evidence: on R7, “show ip ospf neighbor” returns “ospfd is not running,” confirming the OSPF process is stopped. On R6, OSPF is healthy and has neighbors on other links, but “show ip ospf interface” shows eth2 (67.1.1.1/8, Area 0.0.0.1) with “Neighbor Count is 0,” while eth0 and eth1 each have a Full adjacency.

Mechanism: with R7 not sending OSPF hellos, R6’s eth2 on segment Y loses its neighbor. Consequently, routes originated by R7 (17.x.x.x loopbacks and 70.0.0.0/8) disappear from the OSPF domain, and the broadcast link 67.0.0.0/8 between R6 and R7 also ceases to be advertised to the rest of the network. This matches R6’s logs of a neighbor down and R1’s loss of R7-region routes.

Localization: Device R7, interface eth0 (67.1.1.2/8) toward R6; on R6 the impacted interface is eth2 (67.1.1.1/8).

Root cause: The OSPF daemon on R7 is not running.

Corrective fix: Start and enable ospfd on R7 (ensure it’s configured to advertise 67.0.0.0/8, 70.0.0.0/8, and the 17.x loopbacks), then verify the R6–R7 adjacency on R6 eth2 reaches Full and that R1 relearns the missing routes.
=== END ===